-- title:
--   Manin's unimodular paths span: every value of a modular symbol is an integral combination of its values on unimodular paths
-- statement:
--   Manin's unimodular-path theorem, in the form that is actually used when one needs to *produce* elements rather than merely detect them.
--
--   Let $\Gamma \subseteq \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbf Z)$ and let $\mathrm{Div}^0(\mathbf P^1(\mathbf Q))$ be the group of degree-zero divisors on the cusps, with $(x,y)$ denoting $[x]-[y]$. A **unimodular path** is a divisor of the form $g \cdot ([0]-[\infty])$ with $g \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbf Z)$, i.e. a path joining $g\cdot 0$ to $g\cdot\infty$; equivalently, a path between two cusps $a/c$ and $b/d$ with $ad-bc=\pm 1$. Manin's continued-fraction trick shows that these generate $\mathrm{Div}^0(\mathbf P^1(\mathbf Q))$ as an abelian group: writing $r = p_m/q_m$ as the last convergent of its continued fraction expansion, the successive convergents $p_{i-1}/q_{i-1}$ and $p_i/q_i$ satisfy $p_iq_{i-1}-p_{i-1}q_i = \pm 1$, so the path from $\infty$ to $r$ telescopes into finitely many unimodular pieces.
--
--   The statement here is the consequence of that for a modular symbol. If
--   $$\varphi \in \mathrm{Hom}_\Gamma\big(\mathrm{Div}^0(\mathbf P^1(\mathbf Q)),\ \mathrm{Sym}^n(R^2)\big)$$
--   then applying $\varphi$ to such a decomposition expresses **every** value $\varphi(x,y)$ as a $\mathbf Z$-linear combination of the values of $\varphi$ on unimodular paths:
--   $$\varphi(x,y) \in \Big\langle\ \varphi\big(g\cdot 0,\ g\cdot\infty\big)\ :\ g \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbf Z)\ \Big\rangle_{\mathbf Z}.$$
--
--   Note that the subgroup on the right is generated by the values of the *same* $\varphi$, so no auxiliary object appears in the statement, and no equivariance is used in it — the equivariance of $\varphi$ enters only in the proof, and only through $\mathrm{Div}^0$.
--
--   **Relation to the vanishing form.** This statement strictly implies `MTT.Cohomology.manin_generation` (if all the generators vanish, the subgroup is trivial and $\varphi = 0$), but it is genuinely stronger, and the difference matters. The vanishing form says only that a class is *determined* by its unimodular values; it gives an injection into a finite-dimensional coordinate space but says nothing about which coefficients actually occur. The spanning form says the values are *built from* the unimodular values, which is what lets one bound the subgroup of $R$ generated by all coefficients of all values of a single class — a finiteness statement no injectivity result can supply, and the input to flat base change.
-- source:
--   Manin, Parabolic points and zeta functions of modular curves, Izv. Akad. Nauk SSSR Ser. Mat. 36 (1972), 19-66, §1.5-1.7; Merel, Universal Fourier expansions of modular forms, in: On Artin's conjecture for odd 2-dimensional representations, LNM 1585 (1994), §1; Ash-Stevens, Modular forms in characteristic l and special values of their L-functions, Duke Math. J. 53 (1986), §4.

import Definitions.Def_MTT_Cohomology
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Flat.Basic
set_option autoImplicit false
noncomputable section
open scoped BigOperators TensorProduct
open MTT.Cohomology

theorem MTT.Cohomology.manin_generation_span
    {N n : ℕ} {R : Type*} [CommRing R] (φ : Hc N n R) (x y : Cusp) :
    φ.val (x, y) ∈ AddSubgroup.closure
      (Set.range fun g : Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup (Fin 2) ℤ =>
        φ.val (cuspAct g ((0 : ℚ) : Cusp), cuspAct g OnePoint.infty)) := by sorry
